Given, \[\phi(x,y,z)=xy^{2}+yz^{2}+zx^{2}\] Point: \[P=(2,-1,1)\] Direction vector: \[\mathbf{p}=\hat{i}+2\hat{j}+2\hat{k}\] The directional derivative in the direction of \(\mathbf{p}\) is \[D_{\mathbf{p}}\phi
=
\nabla \phi \cdot \hat{u}\] where \(\hat{u}\) is the unit vector along \(\mathbf{p}\). Compute the gradient: \[\frac{\partial \phi}{\partial x}=y^{2}+2zx, \frac{\partial \phi}{\partial y}=2xy+z^{2}, \frac{\partial \phi}{\partial z}=2yz+x^{2}\] Hence, \[\nabla \phi
=
(y^{2}+2zx)\hat{i}
+
(2xy+z^{2})\hat{j}
+
(2yz+x^{2})\hat{k}\] At the point \((2,-1,1)\): \[\nabla \phi(2,-1,1)
=
\left[(-1)^{2}+2(1)(2)\right]\hat{i}
+
\left[2(2)(-1)+1^{2}\right]\hat{j}
+
\left[2(-1)(1)+2^{2}\right]\hat{k}= 5\hat{i}-3\hat{j}+2\hat{k}\] Now compute the unit vector along \(\mathbf{p}\): \[|\mathbf{p}|
=
\sqrt{1^{2}+2^{2}+2^{2}}
=
3\] \[\hat{u}
=
\frac{1}{3}
(\hat{i}+2\hat{j}+2\hat{k})\] Therefore, \[D_{\mathbf{p}}\phi
=
(5\hat{i}-3\hat{j}+2\hat{k})
\cdot
\frac{1}{3}
(\hat{i}+2\hat{j}+2\hat{k})\] \[=
\frac{1}{3}
\left(5-6+4\right)
=
\frac{3}{3}
=
1\] \[\boxed{\text{(a) 1}}\]

questions.question:
Consider the two continuous-time signals defined below: \[x_{1}(t)=
\begin{cases}
|t|, &amp; -1 \leq t \leq 1\\
0, &amp; \text{Otherwise}
\end{cases}\] \[x_{2}(t)=
\begin{cases}
1-|t|, &amp; -1 \leq t \leq 1\\
0, &amp; \text{Otherwise}
\end{cases}\] These signals are sampled with a sampling period of \(T=0.25\) seconds to obtain discrete-time signals \(x_{1}[n]\) and \(x_{2}[n]\), respectively. Which one of the following statements is true?

questions.explanation:
The given continuous-time signals are \[x_{1}(t)=|t|,\qquad -1\le t\le 1\] \[x_{2}(t)=1-|t|,\qquad -1\le t\le 1\] <img src="img/762c3a364a8bc3fb.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,420px)"> Sampling with period \[T=0.25\,\text{s}\] gives the samples \[x_{1}[n]=\{1,0.75,0.5,0.25,0,0.25,0.5,0.75,1\}\] \[x_{2}[n]=\{0,0.25,0.5,0.75,1,0.75,0.5,0.25,0\}\] Energy of a discrete-time signal is \[E=\sum |x[n]|^{2}.\] Hence, \[E_{x_{1}}
=1^{2}+0.75^{2}+0.5^{2}+0.25^{2}+0^{2}+0.25^{2}+0.5^{2}+0.75^{2}+1^{2}
=3.75\] \[E_{x_{2}}
=0^{2}+0.25^{2}+0.5^{2}+0.75^{2}+1^{2}+0.75^{2}+0.5^{2}+0.25^{2}+0^{2}
=2.75.\] Therefore, \[E_{x_{1}}&gt;E_{x_{2}}.\] Observe that the largest-valued samples of \(x_{1}[n]\) occur at both ends, whereas \(x_{2}[n]\) has zeros at both ends. Since energy depends on the sum of squared samples, the two additional unit-valued samples make the energy of \(x_{1}[n]\) greater than that of \(x_{2}[n]\). \[\boxed{\text{(a) The energy of }x_{1}[n]\text{ is greater than the energy of }x_{2}[n].}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Energy of a discrete-time signal is \(\displaystyle E=\sum_{n=-\infty}^{\infty}|x[n]|^{2}\).</li></ul></div>

Given, \[X(\omega)=\frac{1}{(10+j\omega)^2}.\] Using the Fourier transform pair, \[e^{-at}u(t)
\;\Longleftrightarrow\;
\frac{1}{a+j\omega},\] and the differentiation property, \[te^{-at}u(t)
\;\Longleftrightarrow\;
\frac{1}{(a+j\omega)^2}.\] Hence, \[x(t)=te^{-10t}u(t).\] At \(t=1\), \[x(1)=e^{-10}.\] Therefore, \[|\ln x(1)|=|\ln(e^{-10})|=|-10|=10.\] Thus, \[\boxed{10.0}\]

Given : \[\frac{dx_1(t)}{dt}=x_2(t)-x_1(t)\] \[\frac{dx_2(t)}{dt}=x_1(t)-x_2(t)\] The state-space model of the above equation is \[\begin{bmatrix}
\dot{x}_1\\
\dot{x}_2
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
-1 &amp; 1\\
1 &amp; -1
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
x_1\\
x_2
\end{bmatrix}\] \[A=
\begin{bmatrix}
-1 &amp; 1\\
1 &amp; -1
\end{bmatrix}\] \[[sI-A]
=
\begin{bmatrix}
s+1 &amp; -1\\
-1 &amp; s+1
\end{bmatrix}\] \[\phi(s)
=
[sI-A]^{-1}
=
\frac{\operatorname{Adj}[sI-A]}
{|sI-A|}\] \[\operatorname{Adj}[sI-A]
=
\begin{bmatrix}
s+1 &amp; 1\\
1 &amp; s+1
\end{bmatrix}\] \[|sI-A|
=
(s+1)^2-1
=
s^2+2s\] \[[sI-A]^{-1}
=
\frac{1}{s^2+2s}
\begin{bmatrix}
s+1 &amp; 1\\
1 &amp; s+1
\end{bmatrix}\] \[[sI-A]^{-1}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
\dfrac{s+1}{s(s+2)} &amp;
\dfrac{1}{s(s+2)}
\\ \dfrac{1}{s(s+2)} &amp;
\dfrac{s+1}{s(s+2)}
\end{bmatrix}\] \[[sI-A]^{-1}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
\dfrac{1}{2s}+\dfrac{1}{2(s+2)}
&amp;
\dfrac{1}{2s}-\dfrac{1}{2(s+2)}
\\ \dfrac{1}{2s}-\dfrac{1}{2(s+2)}
&amp;
\dfrac{1}{2s}+\dfrac{1}{2(s+2)}
\end{bmatrix}\] Taking inverse Laplace transform, \[\phi(t)
=
\frac{1}{2}
\begin{bmatrix}
1+e^{-2t} &amp; 1-e^{-2t}
\\
1-e^{-2t} &amp; 1+e^{-2t}
\end{bmatrix}\] \[x(t)
=
\phi(t)x(0)
=
\frac{1}{2}
\begin{bmatrix}
1+e^{-2t} &amp; 1-e^{-2t}
\\
1-e^{-2t} &amp; 1+e^{-2t}
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
x_1(0)
\\
x_2(0)
\end{bmatrix}\] \[x(t)
=
\frac{1}{2}
\begin{bmatrix}
x_1(0)+x_2(0)+e^{-2t}[x_1(0)-x_2(0)]
\\ x_1(0)+x_2(0)+e^{-2t}[x_2(0)-x_1(0)]
\end{bmatrix}\] \[x_f
=
\lim_{t\to\infty}x(t)
=
\frac{1}{2}
\begin{bmatrix}
x_1(0)+x_2(0)
\\ x_1(0)+x_2(0)
\end{bmatrix}\] \[x_{1f}=x_{2f}&lt;\infty\] \[\boxed{(C)\;\;x_{1f}=x_{2f}&lt;\infty}\]

Given, \[q=1\,\text{nC}=10^{-9}\,\text{C}, \qquad \text{located at (0,0,0.2)}\] <span class="missing-fig">[figure unavailable]</span> The \(xy\)-plane \((z=0)\) is a conducting ground plane. Using the method of images, the image charge is \[-q=-1\,\text{nC}, \qquad \text{located at (0,0,-0.2)}\] We need the electric field at \[P(0,0,0.1)\] <strong>Field due to the real charge</strong><br/>Distance from the real charge to \(P\): \[R_1=0.2-0.1=0.1\,\text{m}\] Hence, \[E_1=\frac{1}{4\pi\varepsilon_0}\frac{q}{R_1^2}
=
8.99\times10^9\cdot\frac{10^{-9}}{(0.1)^2}
=
899.1\,\text{V/m}\] Since \(P\) lies below the positive charge, the field is along \(-\hat z\): \[\vec E_1=-899.1\,\hat z\] <strong>Field due to the image charge</strong><br/>Distance from the image charge to \(P\): \[R_2=0.1-(-0.2)=0.3\,\text{m}\] Hence, \[E_2=\frac{1}{4\pi\varepsilon_0}\frac{|q|}{R_2^2}
=
8.99\times10^9\cdot\frac{10^{-9}}{(0.3)^2}
=
99.9\,\text{V/m}\] Since the image charge is negative, the field at \(P\) is directed towards it, i.e. along \(-\hat z\): \[\vec E_2=-99.9\,\hat z\] <strong>Total electric field</strong><br/>\[\vec E=\vec E_1+\vec E_2
=
-(899.1+99.9)\hat z\] \[\vec E=-999.0\,\hat z\] Therefore, \[\boxed{D}\]

For a toroidal core, \[L=\frac{\mu N_p^2 A}{l}\] where \[A=\pi r^2,\qquad l=2\pi R\] \[L=\frac{\mu N_p^2 \pi r^2}{2\pi R}
=\frac{\mu N_p^2 r^2}{2R}\] Given, \[i_p=I\sin \omega t\] \[v_p=L\frac{di_p}{dt}
=L I\omega \cos \omega t\] Comparing with \[v_p=V\cos \omega t\] \[V=L I\omega\] \[V=\frac{\mu N_p^2 r^2}{2R}I\omega\] \[R=\frac{\mu I r^2 N_p^2\omega}{2V}\] Hence, the correct option is \[\boxed{D}\]

At original condition, \[I_{a1}=10\,\text{A}\] \[E_{1}=V-I_{a1}R_{a}=200-10(1)=190\,\text{V}\] When torque becomes \(1.44\) times, \[T_{2}=1.44T_{1}\] For a DC series motor with linear magnetization, \[T\propto I_{a}^{2}\] \[I_{a2}^{2}=1.44I_{a1}^{2}\] \[I_{a2}=1.2I_{a1}=1.2(10)=12\,\text{A}\] \[E_{2}=V-I_{a2}R_{a}=200-12(1)=188\,\text{V}\] For a DC series motor, \[E\propto \phi N\] Since \(\phi \propto I_{a}\), \[\frac{E_{2}}{E_{1}}
=
\frac{I_{a2}}{I_{a1}}\times\frac{N_{2}}{N_{1}}\] \[N_{2}
=
\frac{188}{190}\times\frac{10}{12}\times1000\] \[N_{2}=824.56\,\text{rpm}\] Therefore, nearest integer speed is \[\boxed{825}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>For a DC series motor with linear magnetization, \(\phi \propto I_{a}\).</li><li>Torque relation is \(T\propto \phi I_{a}\), hence \(T\propto I_{a}^{2}\).</li><li>Back emf relation is \(E\propto \phi N\).</li></ul></div> <div class="sol-block sol-mistakes"><div class="sol-block-title">Mistakes to avoid</div><ul><li>Do not use \(T\propto I_{a}\) for a series motor in the linear region.</li><li>Do not forget that flux changes with armature current.</li><li>Use \(E=V-I_{a}R_{a}\) for motor operation.</li></ul></div>

For a salient pole synchronous motor, the reluctance power is given by \[P_r=\frac{V^2}{2}
\left(\frac{X_d-X_q}{X_dX_q}\right)
\sin 2\delta\] The corresponding reluctance torque is \[T_r=\frac{P_r}{\omega_s}\] Hence, \[T_r\propto \sin 2\delta\] The reluctance torque attains its maximum value when \[\sin 2\delta = 1\] \[2\delta=90^\circ\] \[\delta=45^\circ\] \[\boxed{\text{B}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Reluctance torque exists only in salient pole machines where \(X_d \neq X_q\).</li><li>The reluctance torque component is proportional to \(\sin 2\delta\).</li><li>Maximum value of \(\sin 2\delta\) occurs when \(2\delta=90^\circ\).</li><li>Cylindrical rotor machines do not develop reluctance torque because \(X_d=X_q\).</li></ul></div>

