For a lossless transmission line, the real power transfer equation is \[P
=
\frac{V_1V_2}{X}\sin\delta\] Hence, the steady-state stability limit is \[P_{max}
=
\frac{V_1V_2}{X}\] which occurs at \[\delta=90^\circ\] For a lossy transmission line, the line impedance is \[Z=|Z|\angle\theta\] The real power transfer equation becomes \[P
=
\frac{V_1V_2}{|Z|}
\cos(\theta-\delta)
-
\frac{V_2^2}{|Z|}\cos\theta\] Because of the presence of resistance, the maximum transferable power is reduced compared to the lossless case. Therefore, the steady-state stability limit of a lossy transmission line is \[\boxed{
\text{(B) less than }
\left|
\frac{V_1V_2}{X}
\right|
}\] <div class="sol-block sol-mistakes"><div class="sol-block-title">Mistakes to avoid</div><ul><li>Do not use the lossless-line stability limit directly for lossy lines.</li><li>Resistance affects both magnitude and phase of line impedance.</li><li>Stability limit depends on the maximum transferable real power, not merely impedance magnitude.</li></ul></div>

